<h2 id="tucson-money-calendar">Tucson's money calendar</h2>
<p>Most cities have one busy season. Tucson has several that overlap:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>The Tucson Gem, Mineral &amp; Fossil Showcase</strong> takes over dozens of venues across the city from late January into February. Vendors need help, hotels fill, and rideshare demand climbs.</li>
  <li><strong>Winter visitors</strong> arrive from October and stay through April, filling Oro Valley, Green Valley, SaddleBrooke and the Catalina Foothills.</li>
  <li><strong>February and March events</strong> like the Tucson Rodeo (La Fiesta de los Vaqueros) and the Tucson Festival of Books on the UA campus bring big crowds.</li>
  <li><strong>The university calendar</strong> brings tens of thousands of students in August and sends them home in May.</li>
  <li><strong>Fall</strong> brings El Tour de Tucson cycling in November and the All Souls Procession downtown.</li>
</ul>
<p>Year-round demand comes from big employers like Raytheon (RTX), Davis-Monthan Air Force Base, Banner–University Medical Center and the university itself, plus a large retiree population that pays for help around the house.</p>

